Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `state_name`: state‑level grouping for the counts  
• `day_of_week` / `day_of_week_name`: identify weekends (1 = Sunday, 7 = Saturday)  
• `atmospheric_conditions_1_name`: primary weather condition; look for “Clear” or “Rain”  
• `atmospheric_conditions_2_name` (optional): a second weather code if the first one is “Other/Unknown”  
• `year_of_crash`: restrict to 2016 (every row in this table is already 2016)  

Typical filter conditions you will need  
• `day_of_week IN (1,7)`  or  `LOWER(day_of_week_name) IN ('saturday','sunday')`  
• `LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear'`            → clear–weather crashes  
• `LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'`             → rainy‑weather crashes  

Below are ten exploratory → analytical queries that gradually build up to the final answer.  
Each query is limited to 20 rows (except the final one) so you can inspect results quickly.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Look at all distinct primary weather descriptions to know the exact spellings.
SELECT DISTINCT atmospheric_conditions_1_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Sample a few weekend crashes to verify “Rain” and “Clear” are present.
SELECT state_name,
       day_of_week_name,
       atmospheric_conditions_1_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
  AND LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) IN ('rain','clear')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Count weekend crashes by weather condition (top 20 for a quick check).
SELECT LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) AS weather,
       COUNT(*) AS crashes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
GROUP BY weather
ORDER BY crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Weekend “Clear” crashes by state (small sample).
SELECT state_name,
       COUNT(*) AS clear_weekend_crashes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
  AND LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear'
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY clear_weekend_crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Weekend “Rain” crashes by state (small sample).
SELECT state_name,
       COUNT(*) AS rain_weekend_crashes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
  AND LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'
GROUP BY state_name
ORDER BY rain_weekend_crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Combine Clear & Rain counts for weekends using conditional aggregation.
SELECT state_name,
       SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS clear_cnt,
       SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS rain_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
GROUP BY state_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Add an absolute difference column and show a quick preview.
SELECT state_name,
       clear_cnt,
       rain_cnt,
       ABS(clear_cnt - rain_cnt) AS diff_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT state_name,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS clear_cnt,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS rain_cnt
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
  WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
  GROUP BY state_name
)
ORDER BY diff_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Check if any state has zero clear or rain crashes (data‑quality inspection).
SELECT state_name,
       clear_cnt,
       rain_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT state_name,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS clear_cnt,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS rain_cnt
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
  WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
  GROUP BY state_name
)
WHERE clear_cnt = 0 OR rain_cnt = 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Same as #7 but use state_number instead of state_name (optional validation).
SELECT state_number,
       state_name,
       ABS(
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) -
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
       ) AS diff_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)
GROUP BY state_number, state_name
ORDER BY diff_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: FINAL ANSWER – Top 3 states with the largest weekend difference
--                 between rainy and clear crashes in 2016 (includes the differences).
SELECT state_name,
       clear_cnt,
       rain_cnt,
       ABS(clear_cnt - rain_cnt) AS difference
FROM (
  SELECT state_name,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'clear' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS clear_cnt,
         SUM(CASE WHEN LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) = 'rain'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS rain_cnt
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2016`
  WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)                          -- weekend only
    AND LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) IN ('clear','rain') -- keep only rows we need
  GROUP BY state_name
)
ORDER BY difference DESC
LIMIT 3;
```
